<Layout
  title="Instinct - Hardware - Rycode"
  description="The custom Instinct GPU is based on the MI450 architecture, with Venice EPYC CPUs, ROCm, and Helios. MI300 and MI350 are the series Meta already runs."
  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Hardware · Chips</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">Instinct</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">Instinct is AMD's line of AI chips. The new chip named in the deal is a custom GPU, or graphics processing unit, based on the MI450 architecture, meaning it is built on AMD's MI450 chip design. Meta already runs the MI300 and MI350 series. The purchase is on the <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/meta/">Meta stack</a>.</p>
    <ChipFigure src="/ai/hardware/chips/instinct.jpg" alt="AMD Instinct MI350-series accelerator module render: the chip package in the center, with blue and orange compute silicon between rows of memory stacks, on a black circuit board." caption="An AMD Instinct MI350-series module." credit="Image: AMD, via TechNews" />
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">MI450</h2>
    <p>On 24 February 2026 AMD said the first deployment uses a custom Instinct GPU based on the MI450 architecture, for Meta's workloads at gigawatt scale. A gigawatt is a billion watts, a common way to size data centers by the power they draw. The GPU is paired with 6th Gen AMD EPYC CPUs codenamed Venice, AMD's ROCm software for running AI code on its GPUs, and AMD Helios, a rack-scale architecture, meaning the whole server rack is designed as one machine. AMD said shipments for the first gigawatt begin in the second half of 2026. The full purchase is up to 6 gigawatts of Instinct GPUs.</p>

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">MI300 and MI350</h2>
    <p>The same 24 February 2026 release says Meta already runs millions of AMD EPYC CPUs, AMD's server processors, and significant deployments of Instinct MI300 and MI350 series GPUs.</p>

